<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Pre-travel care for the deployment (Z71.84)</content>. Entry to French Guiana requires proof of yellow fever vaccination, and CDC recommends it for all travelers there; a single dose gives long-lasting protection, and the certificate is valid from ten days after vaccination. He is 44, with no contraindication or precaution. CDC also recommends hepatitis A and hepatitis B vaccines for unvaccinated travelers and typhoid vaccine for long stays. No dengue vaccine is recommended for travelers. [S]<list><item>Yellow fever vaccine today and the international certificate signed.</item><item>Hepatitis A vaccine dose 1 today; dose 2 in six to twelve months.</item><item>Typhoid Vi vaccine today.</item><item>Hepatitis B vaccine (HepB-CpG) dose 1 today; dose 2 at the pre-departure visit.</item><item>Malaria chemoprophylaxis, insect protection and food and water advice at the pre-departure visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Relapsing P. vivax malaria, after radical cure (B51.9)</content>. No relapse four months after radical cure. [S]<list><item>Continue the fever action plan.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Right forearm abscess (L02.413)</content>. Healed after drainage alone. [S]<list><item>No further care.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Yellow fever vaccine: indication, entry requirement, contraindications and precautions</td><td>Once, before travel</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Required for entry to French Guiana; no contraindication; age under 60; dose given and certificate issued</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Hepatitis A, hepatitis B and typhoid vaccines</td><td>Before travel</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Hepatitis A dose 1, typhoid Vi and HepB-CpG dose 1 given</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Rabies pre-exposure vaccination</td><td>Discuss before travel</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Dogs with rabies are uncommon in French Guiana; rabies is present in bats. His duties are shipboard and coastal; after discussion he chose not to have it and knows to seek post-exposure care after any bite or scratch</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Malaria chemoprophylaxis choice</td><td>Before travel</td><td>Due</td><td>To be prescribed at the pre-departure visit</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Relapse surveillance and the fever action plan</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No fever in four months</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>No further malaria relapse in the six months after radical cure. In progress; target 1705-10-13 [S]</item><item>All required and recommended travel vaccines given, with a valid yellow fever certificate, at least ten days before departure. In progress; target 1705-10-13 [S]</item><item>Knows to have a malaria test the same day for any fever, during travel and for months after return. Outcome by 1705-03-31: Carries a fever card and explained the plan back correctly [S]</item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Yellow fever vaccine, live, single dose; certificate (ICVP) issued, valid from ten days after vaccination; Hepatitis A vaccine, adult, dose 1 of 2; Typhoid Vi polysaccharide vaccine; Hepatitis B vaccine (HepB-CpG), dose 1 of 2 [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: CDC Yellow Book: Health Information for International Travel, 2026 edition. Malaria (2026); CDC Yellow Book: Health Information for International Travel, 2026 edition. Yellow Fever (2026); French Guiana (France): travel health destination page (yellow fever and malaria information updated April 2025) (2026); The practice of travel medicine: guidelines by the Infectious Diseases Society of America (Hill DR, et al. Clin Infect Dis 2006;43(12):1499-1539) (2006)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Pre-departure review in about eleven weeks. Next scheduled visit 1705-10-13: pre-departure review: no relapse, malaria chemoprophylaxis, vector, food and water precautions, standby treatment, and the plan on return. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
